\f1  ("one who speaks in parables/riddles") seems to trigger something in God's response. Rather than simplifying, He intensifies - the interpretation is more disturbing than the riddle. The people wanted clarity but got something worse: confirmation that yes, God means exactly what the terrifying metaphor suggested.\
Your observation about the groaning creating a question that "drives the rhetoric far beyond the riddle starting point" points to verse 12. When they ask why Ezekiel groans, his answer isn't about the riddle or interpretation but about the psychological collapse that comes with truly hearing this message: hearts melting, hands going slack, spirits fainting, knees turning to water.\
What evidence should we examine to understand why Ezekiel structures this oracle this way? The complaint about riddling seems to reveal the audience's deflection mechanism - they can dismiss metaphorical language as unclear, but what happens when the plain interpretation is more unbearable than the metaphor?
