Looking at the evidence more carefully, you're right that the forest fire/sword pairing isn't standard. When fire appears in judgment oracles, it typically stands alone (like Amos 1-2's repeated "I will send fire"). When the sword appears, it's usually personified as an agent of judgment but not typically paired with natural disaster imagery. The riddle form might have genuinely puzzled them rather than just annoying them.\
The grammatical point about 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  as "in order that" rather than "because" fundamentally changes verse 9. If we read it as purposive, then cutting off both righteous and wicked isn't collateral damage but serves the goal that "all flesh from south to north" recognize God drew His sword. The comprehensive nature of destruction becomes pedagogical - only total devastation, without the comfort of believing the righteous escape, forces the recognition.\
