\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fire and sword are juxtaposed in Ezekiel 5, where fire is not so much natural disaster as a sack of a city after siege. But even so, interpreting a fire in a forest as the sword which represents war is not at all something one can expect the audience to have immediately leaped to.\
The pedagogical nature of the destruction links to the structure around the reaction of the exiles. The sword is drawn against all flesh (all Israel, from south to north) - and all flesh will see (which may include not just the exiles, but also the surrounding nations), and all flesh will know.\
The confrontation with something the audience did not imagine does seem to be a reason for riddle, metaphor, and embodied sign. And I agree that the physical symptoms describe the body's response when the mind cannot deflect anymore. But this is not just a response to an earthly disaster. It is a response to the holiness and sovereignty of the revealed LORD, before whom all flesh is has weakness describable with such symptoms. All creation is thus weak in comparison. The only question is whether it knows, and gives glory as it ought.\
But that at best only very partially answers your excellent questions with which you ended. Though I would clarify the first of those questions, as we have not yet established the exact path through which all flesh seeing leads to all flesh knowing, so we should not ask the question in a way that makes implicit a particular path. \
With that caution in mind, there is quite a lot of evidence. \
First, there is a tight correspondence between elements of the riddle and elements of the interpretation, in order. So, for instance, this structure allows us to use elements of the riddle as evidence of what is meant by elements of the interpretation. Specifically in this case, drawing the sword corresponds to 'I am about to kindle a fire in you'. Cutting off the righteous and the wicked corresponds to the fire consuming every green tree and every dry tree, and the blazing flame not being extinguished. \
Second, still using this method of correspondence, having drawing the sword in verse 5 has an implicit logical connection with the recognition that the LORD is the LORD, as does the not returning the sword to its sheath. This is perhaps easier to understand in the riddle. There, the knowledge of the LORD comes from 'I have kindled it, it will not be extinguished'.\
Third, we have the purpose clause you noted earlier. The purpose for drawing the sword is that the LORD may cut off both righteous and wicked. The sword, that is, war, is drawn against all flesh from south to north.\
Fourth, we have the repeated phrase, all flesh. All flesh sees - and given that the human agents of war, Babylon, will be physically present, the other nations will be interested in what Babylon does, there is no reason to restrict all flesh from its default wide scope, the nations seeing is by no means foreign to the rest of Ezekiel, that means the nations see as well. The sword is drawn against all flesh from south to north. Note, using the method of correspondence, that every face from south to north will be scorched by the fire. All flesh will know that the LORD is the LORD. The uses of these phrases may be further connected by the end of verse 5, 'it shall not be sheathed again', in that the sovereignty of the LORD in drawing the sword, the purpose in self revelation, and the not relenting until the all encompassing nature of the judgement accomplishes that purpose, have a clear logical connection.\
The exile's predicted reaction to the news of the fulfillment is clearly part of the overall rhetoric, especially given that the original audience for this riddle and the rest of the oracle is the exiles, so the reaction and the rhetoric surrounding it ought to be included in the evidence. Similarly, the rhetorical role of the certainty of fulfillment. Note the knowledge role of hearing news - and the way the reaction goes beyond simple awareness or academic knowledge.\
The language, such as 'cut off', as in earlier chapters of Ezekiel, recalls Leviticus casuistry specifically in terms of individual punishment for sin - as does Ezekiel 18, which teaches personal responsibility and punishment for sin at length using such casuistry.\
This is of course not all the evidence, but this seems to be the heart of the evidence.
