\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
I think that is more or less the answer, yes.\
The exiles are the audience for this oracle, and both by audience and by the nature of the structural shift to the exiles, we have reason to think that a large part of the rhetorical purpose is aimed at them, in all their rebellion, false hopes, and theological misconceptions. So let us examine the larger rhetorical structure.\
The exiles are given a riddle which is quite difficult to interpret themselves. Then, they are given an interpretation which mirrors the riddle. But that interpretation goes against their rebellion - their hopes are dashed, their theology overturned forcefully, their attempts at life independent from the LORD run into the LORD's sovereignty, and they are part of this all flesh that will see and know. To a great degree, that is a hypothesis from earlier chapters, but I think it confirmed by the evidence of this oracle.\
Then, Ezekiel embodies the sign of groaning. Here, we must make a distinction between the rhetoric of the text, and the rhetoric of the original prophetic sign. In the text, the structure where the sign mirrors Ezekiel's complaint is highly relevant. But the oral rather than written original audience would presumably not know that. But they do have the sign, their own question, and the answer to it.\
The interpretation has already predicted that the disaster it warns of will cause knowledge of the LORD. This, despite the rebellion and deflection of the audience. The physical symptoms describe the body's response when the mind cannot deflect anymore; events have overwhelmed. But this is not just a response to an earthly disaster. It is a response to the holiness and sovereignty of the revealed LORD, before whom all flesh is has weakness describable with such symptoms. All creation is thus weak in comparison. The only question is whether it knows, and gives glory as it ought.\
The physical symptoms are, of course, understandable enough given the news of the disaster that will happen. But the structure allows the message of that disaster, the knowledge of the LORD and not just of political and military news, to come through when that time comes. Similarly, along with some of Ezekiel's other oracles, having structured this oracle in this way allows all flesh, the nation of Israel and the other nations, to understand the LORD's purposes, lest they misunderstand the disaster that would befall Jerusalem. This, in turn, allows the LORD's self-revelation.\
Which brings us back to the purpose clause.\
Ezekiel 14 taught that even if a righteous men, such as Noah, Daniel, or Job were in Jerusalem, they could not deliver the city. They could not even deliver their own children. Moreover, when the exiles see the survivors of the fall of Jerusalem, they will be consoled - not because there are survivors, but because the wickedness of the survivors will show that it was not without cause that the LORD acted thus in bringing disaster on Jerusalem.\
Ezekiel 21 comes at the same question from an even more shocking angle. The righteous cannot save the city - the sword, that is war, will be drawn against them. By using language similar to the twisting of correct theology which gives false hope, Ezekiel dashes that false hope to the ground. War comes so that the righteous and the wicked will be cut off. The result will be knowledge of the LORD.\
In addition to false hope surrounding false reasons the LORD has to rescue the city, there was false belief about the LORD's power. Those false reasons the LORD has to rescue the city might combine with the fall of the city to confirm false belief that the LORD is weak. By announcing and explaining in advance intent to cut off both righteous and wicked, the LORD prevents this misinterpretation of future events. Instead, cutting off both righteous and wicked is a demonstration of the LORD's sovereignty. "Then everyone will recognize that I am the LORD; I have kindled it (that is, the fire devouring every green tree and every dry tree that corresponds to war); it will not be extinguished.
