\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Your analysis reveals how the comprehensive destruction functions as theological prophylaxis against future misinterpretation. The advance announcement that both righteous and wicked will be cut off prevents two opposite but equally false conclusions when Jerusalem falls: either that the "righteous" must have had hidden sins (maintaining the theology of retribution while denying reality), or that YHWH was too weak to protect His people (maintaining their innocence while denying divine sovereignty).\
The structural progression you've traced shows sophisticated rhetorical strategy:\
The riddle's difficulty forces the exiles to struggle and fail at interpretation. When the plain interpretation comes, it's worse than anything they imagined - not because God is clarifying but because He's revealing intent they couldn't conceive. The groaning then embodies what happens when this truth breaks through all deflection mechanisms.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The false theology being dismantled seems to be: - Righteous presence guarantees city's survival (refuted by Ezek 14) - God must save the righteous if He's powerful (refuted by Ezek 21) - Destruction means either hidden sin or divine weakness (prevented by advance announcement) </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The connection between Ezekiel's complaint about being dismissed as a riddler and his groaning sign is striking. His complaint gets transformed into prophecy - they dismiss his riddles, so God gives them interpretation that causes physical collapse. The very accusation ("mere riddler") becomes part of the oracle's structure.\
The phrase "all flesh" expanding from Israel to nations changes everything. When Babylon destroys Jerusalem including the righteous, the nations won't think "their god was weak" or "Jerusalem must have been secretly wicked." They'll recognize deliberate divine action because it was announced beforehand with this specific comprehensive scope.\
But this raises a question about the temporal structure. The exiles will hear about Jerusalem's fall and react with these physical symptoms. Yet the oracle is given to them now, before the fall. How does the advance warning function rhetorically for the exiles themselves, as opposed to "all flesh" more broadly?
