\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It occurs to me that one function of the "all flesh" language is to include the exiles - but also to lump them together with Jerusalem, the nations, or both.\
The prophesy is against the land of Israel, and part of that recognition of deliberate divine action starts with saying to the land "I am against you". That impacts differently given that the audience is people already exiled from the land - but they do seem to have hoped to go home soon. \
As for the temporal structure, announcing "I am against you" before the human agents of that attack act (for human reasons, but also because the LORD in his sovereignty spoke, and so thus it happened) does seem to be key. The argument is much stronger given the prediction in advance; as you say, they'll recognize deliberate divine action because it was announced beforehand with this specific comprehensive scope.\
But your question was about the intersection of exile specific rhetoric and temporal structure. Ezekiel is drawn into the structure - his complaint with respect to riddles corresponds in the interpretation to the embodied sign of groaning. The exiles are drawn in. From their seeing the sign of groaning, to asking, to hearing the interpretation of the groaning, to melting, they will participate in beholding that it is coming and will be fulfilled, and that it has come and was fulfilled. The LORD speaks a word, and it happens.
