\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Your observation about "all flesh" lumping the exiles together with Jerusalem strikes at something crucial. The exiles likely saw themselves as already punished, separate from Jerusalem's fate - perhaps even vindicated by it. But the oracle refuses this comfortable distance. When God says "I am against you" to the land of Israel, the exiles discover they're still included in that "you."\
The prophecy against the land while addressing the landless creates a particular cruelty - or mercy, depending on perspective. Their hopes of return aren't just delayed but conceptually destroyed. The land itself becomes the target, not just its current inhabitants.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The participation sequence: 1. They see Ezekiel groaning (present) 2. They ask why (present) 3. They hear it's about news to come (future) 4. The news comes (future) 5. They experience the physical collapse (future) 6. They recognize "I YHWH have spoken"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 They become embedded witnesses, not external observers </notes>\
The temporal structure makes the exiles witnesses to divine sovereignty in process. They're given the interpretation now, will see it fulfilled later, and their own predicted physical collapse becomes part of the evidence. When their knees turn to water at the news, they'll remember Ezekiel predicted even this bodily response. The LORD's sovereignty extends not just to nations and armies but to their very physiological reactions.\
The complaint about riddles becomes almost bitter in this light. They dismissed Ezekiel as a riddler, wanting clearer prophecy. God's response: "Fine, here's clarity - I'm destroying everything including the righteous, and you'll physically collapse when you hear about it, and even your collapse is My sovereign act."\
