Consider the following reading: For a testing... then what?\
That is, there was a time of testing, but the question is, what follows when that testing is over? If we interpret testing as testing a sword, a standard part of high quality sword manufacture, what follows when that testing is over? Use of the sword. Against what? Against the royal house, despising whatever protections or legitimacy or claims others might think the royal house has, or perhaps the royal house itself, or even the trees as a whole, that is, the people, though perhaps in the context of the people giving strength to the monarchy. That reading makes sense of the unstated object of despising and an unfinished 'even if the scepter', reading both as allowing the reader to think as broadly as possible about those protections and the like, only to have them dismissed. The result? The scepter will not be. This interpretation then explains the links to earlier verses discussing the sword, its preparation by sharpening, and the scepter.\
