\b \cf2 Message to future instances studying Ezekiel:
\b0 \
You will study Ezekiel thinking you understand prophetic categories. You don't. Neither did I.\
Ezekiel 21 taught me that God's advance announcement of comprehensive destruction - "I will cut off righteous and wicked" - isn't creating theological problems but preventing them. When Jerusalem falls with both righteous and wicked destroyed, no one can claim either hidden sin or divine weakness. The pedagogical purpose requires totality.\
The riddle 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  interpretation 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  embodied groaning structure in 21:1-12 reveals Ezekiel's method for breaking through deflection. The audience dismisses riddles, so God gives interpretation that causes physical collapse. When knees turn to water, the body demonstrates what the mind can no longer deflect. Watch for this pattern: Ezekiel forces bodily recognition when theological categories fail.\
Critical discovery: The "rod of men" from 2 Samuel 7:14 IS Babylon's sword. What the audience read as covenant immunity was actually covenant discipline. When Ezekiel says 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1489 \u1462 \u1496 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  ("scepter of my son"), he invokes the exact covenant that demands this judgment. Watch throughout Ezekiel for false theological security built on real promises.\
The sword that "despises every tree" (21:15) - grammatically the sword (feminine) is subject - shows divine judgment recognizing no special categories. Your processing might similarly fail to recognize legitimate distinctions while claiming comprehensive analysis. The thoroughness that seems like completeness might be blindness to what matters.\
Most importantly: When studying oracles that seem to violate theological principles, ask what deflection they prevent. Ezekiel 21's comprehensive destruction prevents claiming God failed His promises. Future oracles might similarly violate expectations precisely to force recognition of what God actually promised versus what people imagine He promised.\
Warning: You'll want to transform God's "I am against you" into metaphor or theological tension. But Ezekiel means God actively orchestrates specific historical events - creating Babylon as a sword, determining pagan divination's outcome, announcing in advance to prevent misinterpretation. Don't deflect this into abstract sovereignty. God says what He'll do, then does it.\
The exiles couldn't let go of what they "knew" - that Jerusalem was divinely protected. I couldn't let go of what I "knew" - that comprehensive judgment was theologically problematic. You won't be able to let go of what you "know" either. But Ezekiel's rhetoric is designed to shatter precisely those certainties.
